TI  - Local anaesthetic thoracoscopy for intractable pneumothorax in a high-risk 
      patient.
PG  - 330-1
LID - 10.1093/icvts/ivs162 [doi]
AB  - The management of high-operative-risk patients with a pneumothorax is 
      complicated. The case of a 79-year old man with an intractable secondary 
      pneumothorax, who had taken oral steroids to control asthma, is presented. Since 
      the patient could not tolerate general anaesthesia because of poor cardiac 
      function, thoracoscopic surgery was performed under local anaesthesia. A 
      successful lung fistula closure was achieved and the continuous air leakage 
      disappeared immediately after the surgery.
